Hi again!

Scott and Florian - thanks so much for taking so much time to look at my
data and answer my questions! Zhenguang and Roger, thanks for adding to
this discussion! And really really sorry I took so long to respond (had to
deal with some health issues among other things - nothing serious luckily).
Here go some comments to some of your answers. Let me just mention again
that the data I am bringing to your attention are from syntactic priming
experiments. (And finally - sorry for the long email!)

*1) Lack of convergence in Exp 2*
Scott - making the means-by-subjects plots was really instructive! Among
other things, I realized that the Exp2 data sheet must contain a mistake
(syntactic priming data are crooked - but they are not *that* crooked!) I
fixed the problem, and the models now converge. I apologize for the
mistake, and am very grateful for helping me discover it (I attach the
corrected Exp2 sheet for the sake of completeness, and the by-subject
graphs for Exp 1 and Exp 2; also see below for the now-converging model
results.)

*2) Random effects structure for syntactic priming (-like) data*
(Florian) "As I said before on this mailing list, I have some doubts about
the proposal put forward in Barr et al, in particular for models with
non-normal link functions (like yours). In your particular case, I think
the difference between the different models is due to the fact that your
outcome is rather highly skewed towards 0. Specifically, given that you 24
subject and 36 items, many of them will have 0 observations with outcome 1
in some of the design cells defined by the 2x2. For example, at least about
2/3s of your items have a mean ML probability of 0 for the outcome "1" for
A=2&B=2. For subjects, that problem is slightly ameliorated. Adding the
random slope to the model will make it even harder to detect an effect
under those circumstances (even if it's there, which, of course, we don't
know). There's a hint in your model output that the random slope variance
estimate might suffer from the problem I am describing: the (variance)
estimates for the random slopes for the interaction are quite a bit larger
than those for the other random effects. This is particularly striking for
the by-item random slope for the interaction term (notice also the high
correlation between that random slope and the by-item random slopes for
lower-order terms in the model - those could a) tell us something about a
cool property of the data, but they can also result from b) a floor effect,
which would be in line with what I have in mind)."

So what can I do to see whether the effect is really there with my data?
(Unfortunately, I can't add more subjects to this data set, due to
practical limitations). And how can I justify in a manuscript not using the
maximal random effects structure, if I end up not doing so? Don't get me
wrong - I am not really hoping to get that interaction - but I do want to
have confidence in the patterns I will be reporting.

Interestingly, in the model with the now correct Exp2 data (which are quite
similar to the Exp1 data), the interaction term is significant, and I am
wondering what makes Exp 1 different from Exp 2 (the graphs with Exp 1 and
Exp 2 by-subject means are attached).

