*3) Variability *
(Scott) "First, the variance captured by the random effects for the
interaction is quite large, which should lead you to expect that people are
varying a lot."

Yes - I have by now done a few syntactic priming experiments, and I know
that people's performance in these experiments tends to be particularly
variable (also evident from Zhenguang's and Roger's emails - I know Roger's
data are not from syntactic priming, but they seem similar). On the other
hand, I have done several series of priming experiments, and within a
series (where experiments are very similar to each other) the overall
patterns are typically very consistent. What I mean by this is that within
an experiment the variability is large, but the means are replicable (e.g.,
about half of the subjects in the sample show the overall effect). Isn't
this replicability something to give me (us) confidence in the patterns we
are finding?

(Florian) "the problem you're having in your data is the reason why we
include in our papers analyses of binomial data after excluding
participants that do not show at least 15% of either outcome."

Thanks very much for this information, and the references! However, I am
wondering about that one, and I am curious what the field things about it.
It seems to depend on the questions you are asking. If you are interested
in a certain property of syntactic priming - *whenever it occurs* - then
yes, excluding subjects not showing it seems appropriate. However, if you
are asking - as in my case - whether priming occurs at all under certain
conditions, the fact that there are some people who respond with only one
or only the other construction (only 1 or only 0 responses throughout)
seems informative, and ultimately I don't think it's right to exclude those
people (although keeping them makes data analyses more challenging). Does
anyone have any thoughts about this?

*4) Published results employing ANOVAs*

> (Me) "Does this mean that all published (e.g. syntactic priming)
> experiments that were analyzed with ANOVAs should be reevaluated?"

(Florian) "Well, on some level that's what I argued in Jaeger (2008), with
the caveat that (lucky us) most results within the .3 < mean proportion <
.7 range are pretty save. Note your data fall *outside *of that range."

I took a quick look at some published results (from some of the most
influential syntactic priming papers), and at least half of the experiments
reported (in what I looked at) had mean proportions (in some conditions)
that were < .3 or > .7. Particularly problematic are papers about priming
of Noun Phrase modifiers (e.g., the red car) versus Relative Clause
modifiers (e.g., the car that's red) - since people hardly, if at all,
produce the latter. I am just thinking that, after all, the existing
theories of how syntactic priming works (which also have broader
implications than just priming) are based on those results, and am
wondering what that implies about our understanding of this phenomenon, and
all it relates to.

*5) Practical questions
5a) Simple main effects*
(Florian) To run a simple effect equivalent, simply remove the main effect
from the model
data ~ A + A:B + (1 + A + A:B  | subj) + (1 + A + A:B | item)
should give you the main effect of A, and two simple effects for B at the
different levels of A.

