Hi Zhenguang,

When you say the "lme results" what are you referring to?  Do you mean the inferences about the fixed effects?  (The random effects parameter estimates are of course different since you have reparameterized the model.)  Releveling doesn't change model class, so the inferences about fixed effects should *not* change -- and as you can see, the log-likelihood of the model is extremely similar with and without releveling.  The differences obtained regarding fixed-effects inferences are almost certainly due to some combination of (i) how the Laplace approximation works for a glmm, together with (ii) issues of numerical precision in finding the parameters that give the optimum Laplace-approximated likelihood.  So I would read nothing into the differences between the two model fits, except that it gives you a sense of the limit of how many decimal places out you should trust the fit of any glmm in lme4 with the kind of dataset you have.

This would also be a good question for R-sig-ME, where there is a higher concentration of experts in the gory details of lme4.

Best

Roger

