From Ariel.Goldberg-qzQkmLjiwf+HXe+LvDLADg at public.gmane.org  Wed Feb 13 01:18:38 2013
From: Ariel.Goldberg-qzQkmLjiwf+HXe+LvDLADg at public.gmane.org (Goldberg, Ariel M)
Date: Wed, 13 Feb 2013 01:18:38 +0000
Subject: Differences between model comparison and residualizing
Message-ID: <D6FB1B594A87EA4E99980F6BC00C9D7F09F221D7@SSVMEXDAG01MB01.tufts.ad.tufts.edu>

Hi Everyone,

I'm investigating the processing of multimorphemic words and am interested in determining whether a suffixed word's root frequency and word frequency contribute to the way it is produced.  These two variables are correlated in my dataset with about r=.7.

The first way that I tried to figure out whether each frequency made an independent contribution was to use model comparison, to see if one frequency measure added to the fit of the model above and beyond the other.  In this comparison I used full random effects.

(1)
#Does root freq contribute above and beyond word freq?
m1=lmer(X~Y+WordFreq                   +(1+WordFreq+RootFreq|Subject)+(1+WordFreq+RootFreq|Word)
m2=lmer(X~Y+WordFreq+RootFreq+(1+WordFreq+RootFreq|Subject)+(1+WordFreq+RootFreq|Word)
anova(m1,m2)

(2)
#Does word freq contribute above and beyond root freq?
m1=lmer(X~Y+RootFreq                   +(1+WordFreq+RootFreq|Subject)+(1+WordFreq+RootFreq|Word)
m2=lmer(X~Y+WordFreq+RootFreq+(1+WordFreq+RootFreq|Subject)+(1+WordFreq+RootFreq|Word)
anova(m1,m2)

When I do this, both of the comparisons are significant -- i.e., adding each frequency measure improves the fit of the model above the other.


Since I wasn't sure if this was the best way to do this, I also took a look at residualizing one frequency against the other, using the command below.
d$rWordFreq=residuals(lm(WordFreq~RootFreq,data=d)

I then used model comparison to see if it contributed significantly above a baseline model containing only the nuisance variables.  The residualized predictor was included as both fixed and random effects.

(3)
#Does WordFreq residualized against RootFreq significantly improve a baseline model?

m1=lmer(X~Y                      +(1+rWordFreq|Subject)+(1+rWordFreq|Word)
m2=lmer(X~Y+rWordFreq+(1+rWordFreq|Subject)+(1+rWordFreq|Word)
anova(m1,m2)

(4)
#Does RootFreq residualized against WordFreq significantly improve a baseline model?

m1=lmer(X~Y                     +(1+rRootFreq|Subject)+(1+rRootFreq |Word)
m2=lmer(X~Y+rRootFreq+(1+rRootFreq|Subject)+(1+rRootFreq |Word)
anova(m1,m2)


When I residualize root frequency against word frequency, the residualized predictor is significant in the model comparison.  The confusing thing is that when I residualize word frequency against root frequency, rRootFreq is not significant (e.g., p=.13)

I'm confused that the model comparison approach (analyses 1 & 2) produce different results from the residualization approach (3 & 4).  It seems to me (intuitively) that the two analyses are doing the same thing, giving as much variance to the first variable as possible and then seeing if what's left in the second variable significantly accounts for any variance.

Does anybody have any insight as to why these techniques differ and which, if any, is the appropriate test to use?

Thanks!
Ariel

