From: hunter.hatfield-I1m3D+DYSVWlP80pJB477g at public.gmane.org (Hunter Hatfield)
Date: Wed, 13 Feb 2013 20:47:26 +0000
Subject: poisson regression and random effects
Message-ID: <E37BDF470EF29B4CA4C86E32B6936A13126DB24D@ITS-EXM-P05.registry.otago.ac.nz>

Good afternoon everyone,

I wanted to bounce an idea off of the group, or really ask for clarification/confirmation. A student I am working with is counting relative clauses in a series of texts in different varieties of Arabic. For this, we're using mixed effects Poisson regression. I have only worked with linear and binomial myself, though I am not sure our issue is unique to the Poisson models. Anyway, her data format for a long time looked something like:

Count        RC_Type       Text              Cat1
25            SU                Book1            Y
15            SU                Book1            N
12            SU                Book1            Y
9              SU                Book1            N
18            DO                Book1            Y
16            DO                Book1            N
22            SU                Book2            Y
17            SU                Book2            N
11            SU                Book2            Y
10            SU                Book2            N
15            DO                Book2            Y
14            DO                Book2            N

etc.

The key here is that there are twice as many rows for the Subject (SU) RC-Type as the Direct Object (DO) RC-Type and all other RC-Types (not listed in sample data). The reason for this is that originally, Subjects were broken down into transitive and intransitive subjects, so that the data was balanced for all types. However, for a later question, we were just interested in all Subjects and whether there were significantly more of them than not. When you look at raw counts, those for the SU relative-clause type were double those of DO, but when she ran the Poisson regression, SU was no higher than DO in the model predictions. The model was of the form:

model1 <- lmer(Count ~ RC_Type + (1|Text), data = data, family=poisson)

I at first interpreted this as showing the great benefits of handling random effects because there must be a lot of variation between texts, which when accounted for showed that SU was no more likely than DO. However, when she looked at each text individually, SU was always higher in Count than DO relative clauses. I then noticed the imbalanced data and asked her to restructure it so that the formerly transitive and intransitive subject lines were added together into a true single SU count. The restructured data then looked like:

Count        RC_Type       Text              Cat1
37            SU                Book1            Y
24            SU                Book1            N
18            DO                Book1            Y
16            DO                Book1            N
33            SU                Book2            Y
27            SU                Book2            N
15            DO                Book2            Y
14            DO                Book2            N

Running the same model, SU is now significantly more likely than DO, as the raw data suggested. So, in short, it appears that the random intercepts for Text (1 | Text) were inappropriately decreasing SU counts due to its being broken-up into multiple rows. That the random intercepts where removing the difference between DO and SU was also confirmed by putting in a dummy category with no variation (1 | Dummy) with the first data. In that case, SU was significantly higher than DO.

What I think this all shows is my lack of understanding of how the calculations work. I was wondering if this vague account ("inappropriate" changes with random effects on the first data format) indeed seemed reasonable to others and if someone might explain what's going on if possible.

Thank you. This is sample data, as I do not have the actual in front of me, but I hope it contains all the key structure.

Hunter Hatfield

From scottuba-Re5JQEeQqe8AvxtiuMwx3w at public.gmane.org  Wed Feb 13 21:14:28 2013
From: scottuba-Re5JQEeQqe8AvxtiuMwx3w at public.gmane.org (Scott Jackson)
Date: Wed, 13 Feb 2013 16:14:28 -0500
Subject: Re: poisson regression and random effects
In-Reply-To: <E37BDF470EF29B4CA4C86E32B6936A13126DB24D@ITS-EXM-P05.registry.otago.ac.nz>
References: <E37BDF470EF29B4CA4C86E32B6936A13126DB24D@ITS-EXM-P05.registry.otago.ac.nz>
Message-ID: <CA+tkQ_M1mREhT9LozFWQ5pYvfmA9z+eB31mfDqVVZXcpwajsnA@mail.gmail.com>

Hi Hunter,

I'm not especially experienced with Poisson models, either, but I
think I can see what's going on here.  I think it helps if you think
of each row as an "observation."

The problem is that by splitting up the subject rows, you were
essentially reducing the Count by half.  So if you just think about RC
Type predicting Count, it looks like some of the SU values are higher
than the DO, and some are lower, so overall, no difference if you just
try to use the RC Type column to predict the Count column.  The model
doesn't "know" that it should be summing the Counts for SU across
conditions!  For that matter, it looks like they are also split up by
"Cat1", but that's not in the model.  Why not?

To put this another way, I think all the Poisson model is doing is
supplying you with the "log" link function.  It doesn't automatically
adjust the counts when you have multiple rows per condition.  So if
(for example) you're not putting Cat1 in the model, I think you should
just sum all the counts for each RC type for each Text.  Each Text
will have two rows, so it's still meaningful and appropriate to model
the Text as a random effect, but what you care about is whether SU has
higher counts than DO, so you should compare those (summed) cells.

Now, if you want to show that there's a main effect of RC Type with
(or without) some interaction with Cat1, then you could break them
apart by Cat1 (four rows per Text), and you should center those
variables (or do sum coding of contrasts, or some other equivalent
thing) and include the factorial design in the model, to test for a
main effect.  But if you aren't testing anything having to do with
Cat1, I would suggest just summing the Counts down to two rows per
Text (one for SU and one for DO)

Finally, you may want to at least try to model the random slope of RC
Type by Text (i.e., (1+RC_Type | Text) ), since that allows texts to
vary randomly in the SU vs. DO difference, which seems like a likely
assumption to make.

Hope this helps,
-scott

