>>From what you wrote under 1) above, I thought your models now converge,
correct? Do you have reasonable variance estimates for all random slopes?

Sometimes the data just doesn't allow us to answer our question under
certain standards of confidence. In those cases, we can then decide to
either report nothing or to report what we think we could reasonably
conclude while being clear about the caveats that apply to our data.That is
one of the reasons why I remain skeptical of the hypothesis-testing vs.
model building distinction in the Barr et al paper. The generalization is
that we always have think about what assumptions our analyses are based on.
Practically though this means that I would ask myself whether I'd be
sufficiently convinced by my own data (is the data sufficiently clear to
warrant discussing it in a paper? is the cost of getting more data
sufficiently high to explain why I didn't get more?).

You can bolster your argument by analyzing a subset of the data that is
more suitable for the question you have in mind (e.g., where you have
sufficient variability across subjects). I don't recall the details of your
study, but one thing I have done in the past is to restrict the analyses to
people who produce both structures at least 15% of the time. that doesn't
get rid of the problem of floor or ceiling effects (which can create
spurious interactions or mask real ones), but it ameliorates the problem of
fitting reliable random slopes. Of course, your conclusions are then
conditional on the exclusion of those subjects (though our conclusions are
always conditional on our exclusions, and few people ever test the effect
of their exclusions -- unfortunately).



