=). Questions like the one you're raising was part of why I wrote that
paper (and, I suspect, why it was't really met with enthusiasm by the
reviewers). For many priming phenomena, there are plenty of replications,
some within, some outside that critical range. So for those, I am not so
concerned. But, yes, it's very well possible that there are domain in work
on priming and elsewhere for which only unreliable results exist.

That all being said, I think that the statistical issues we tend to discuss
on this list weigh less in the overall "likelihood to replicate"
consideration. FWIW, my gut feeling is that the problems we discuss order *
roughly *like this:

common bad standards (adding a few more subjects; playing around with
additional predictors; not reporting all relevant analyses 'picking;
Simmons et al., 2011), bias not to report null results or failures to
replicate > orphan result (not yet replicated but also no evidence that it
won't), , failure to consider floor or ceiling effects, using an
intercept-only models (e.g., Baayen, 2004, Baayen et al., 2008) *>>> *using
ANOVA for binomial data outside "ok" range (Jaeger, 2008; Dixon, 2008),
failure to check whether there is variability (without which no effect can
be found), failure to check power (cf. Jaeger et al., 2011) > maximal
random effect structure justified by data rather than full random effect
structure (Barr et al., 2012) > using ANOVA for binomial data inside "ok"
range

Of course, any of these things can have large or small effects.


