Message-ID: <CA+tkQ_OnpmRNqXyqX_-AqvbQvFCC2XtP7gZnB3sUb0-_fK1LAQ@mail.gmail.com>

Thanks for all the interesting discussion! Just a couple of things to
add to Florian's comments:

One thing to keep in mind is that the effects we're interested in are
not guaranteed to be the most important/biggest effects in the
experiment. With so much by-subject variation, you have to wonder what
the driving effects are that would cause several people to respond "0"
to almost every item in the experiment, while other people responded
"1" to almost every item in some of the conditions (esp the 0.5 level
of A).  Maybe it's some kind of individual difference, who knows?  In
contrast, the effects of our experimental manipulations may be rather
small.  There are a few ways of looking at this. One is that you don't
necessarily want to look too closely at individual subjects' data,
since any individual may be too "noisy" with these other, unmeasured
factors to be able to detect anything. One of the purported advantages
of the mixed-effects models is to provide "partial pooling" across
people, in order to get more stable estimates of the population
effects (see Gelman & Hill 2007 for lots of discussion on this).  So
if you look at your by-subject data and don't see many people fitting
the overall pattern, that's not unexpected, and doesn't (necessarily)
mean there's something wrong with your effect.

That said, especially when you have only 24 subjects, it's also very
possible that your effects are highly dependent on one or two
"extreme" subjects.  In other words, some subjects may be too
"influential" on your overall model fit, which is the kind of effect
that an outlier has. There are ways of finding this out (I've posted
before on the influence.ME package, which provides an interesting set
of tools), but even if you find out that one or two people are
completely driving your effect, it's not clear (to me, at least) how
to interpret that for all cases.  One possibility it that you have one
or two people behaving erratically, and the result is a "fluke."
Another possibility is that the effect is stochastically distributed
across the population, and most of the time people show it only very
slightly, so in order to find the effect with a small number of
people, you need to get lucky and get a couple of "extreme" people to
magnify it a bit.

So in the end, you need more data/more studies to get a better
picture, and science rolls on :-)  I understand that adding more
subjects to these experiments may be out, but maybe it's a sign that
future experiments should run more people, whether you want to employ
something like Florian's suggestion or not.  But it also may mean that
future studies aimed at understanding why people are so variable (what
makes a person be a "non-responder", responding "0" to every item?
what makes someone respond almost 100% in the +A conditions?) could
provide a much larger insight about the nature of responses in this
task.  Or maybe a study about the variability of items would be more
helpful. These are all big issues.  I think for your purposes,
reporting things as you've found them here makes sense to me.  The big
effect is the effect of A.  Your models indicate an interaction with
B, but if you poke around more (maybe following Florian's advice, or
trying something else), you may want to qualify it with a caveat.  But
I don't see anything clearly "wrong" with your data or model that
would rule out the possibility that these results are legit.

One practical tip for getting planned contrasts:  fit the uncentered
(i.e., 0 and 1) versions of your factors (as a starting point), and
then walk yourself through computing the coefficients of the
contrasts.  So if you don't center, then the intercept is the -A-B
cell, the effect of +A is the difference between -A-B and +A-B, the
effect of +B is the difference between -A-B and -A+B, and the
interaction is the adjustment of the difference of +/- A for the +B
levels.  Using simple addition that even *I* can manage, you can get
all the estimates for the coefs for any paired comparison that you're
interested in.  Then use the contrast coding or some dummy-coding
method or re-leveling or whatever to get the coefs and their std
errors, but you can always check those estimates against the ones you
hand-calculated, to make sure you've coded things right. Contrast
coding is unintuitive the first few hundred times you do it, at least
in my experience, and doing a double-checking method like this can
help you catch things.

best,
-scott

